COPY public.agentalerts (alertid, createdat, machineid, orderid, defecttype, detectioncount, windowstart, windowend, status, report, completedat) FROM stdin;
1	2026-07-28 23:41:04-07	1	4901	patches	6	2026-07-28 23:40:34-07	2026-07-28 23:41:04-07	done	## Root Cause Investigation Report: Machine 1 Patches Defect Burst\n\n### 1. What the Camera Saw\n\nThe camera detected 6 patches defects with confidence ≥0.80 on Machine 1 during a 19-second window (2026-07-29 06:40:32–06:40:51Z). The detections appeared across 5 distinct image frames at regular intervals: patches_119.jpg (06:40:32), patches_152.jpg (06:40:37), patches_16.jpg (06:40:42), patches_165.jpg (06:40:46), and patches_175.jpg (06:40:51). All detections were tagged to Work Order 4901. This represents a burst of high-confidence signals in rapid succession, distinct from the baseline detection rate.\n\n### 2. Machine and Work-Order Context\n\nWork Order 4901 (eBike T101, Frame Fabrication) was running on Machine Fra-10 (Frame Fabrication) under operator John Gilbert on the night shift. The order status was recorded as in_progress with 9.09% completion (1 of 11 units produced) as of 2026-07-07. No downtime events were recorded on Machine 1 during the burst window or in the 7-day lookback period. No human quality-control defect records exist for patches defects on Machine 1 during this period, meaning the camera detections have not yet been confirmed as defects by QC.\n\n### 3. Most Likely Root Cause (Ranked)\n\n**Hypothesis 1: Vision System Processing Anomaly – HIGH Certainty**\n\nWHY: The first detection (patches_119.jpg at 06:40:32Z) recorded an inference time of 1200.75 ms, approximately 25 times longer than the normal baseline of 41–45 ms. This spike occurred at the exact onset of the burst and is the only inference latency outlier in the sequence. Subsequent frames returned to normal inference times (41–45 ms). This pattern suggests a transient vision system condition—GPU load spike, model uncertainty, or frame buffering delay—that triggered elevated confidence scores on the first frame and then resolved. The burst may reflect the system's response to a temporary processing bottleneck rather than a sustained product defect.\n\nCertainty: HIGH. The inference latency anomaly is directly measured in tool output and temporally coincides with burst onset.\n\n**Hypothesis 2: Real Product Surface Condition on Work Order 4901 – MEDIUM Certainty**\n\nWHY: The 6 detections are distributed across 5 distinct image frames over 19 seconds, showing multi-frame consistency and high confidence scores (0.81–0.87). This temporal clustering and frame diversity suggest a real product condition rather than a single-frame artifact. If the vision system anomaly is ruled out, the pattern points to a systematic issue on the Frame Fabrication machine—worn tooling, material batch defect, misalignment, or setup error—that affected multiple units in rapid succession.\n\nCertainty: MEDIUM. The multi-frame consistency is suggestive, but no human QC records exist to confirm the detections as actual defects, and no maintenance or setup logs are available to pinpoint the cause.\n\n**Hypothesis 3: Lighting or Conveyor Speed Transient – LOW Certainty**\n\nWHY: A temporary change in camera lighting or conveyor speed during the burst window could have altered image quality or product presentation, triggering false-positive clustering in the vision model. However, no environmental or equipment logs are available to support this mechanism.\n\nCertainty: LOW. No supporting data in tool results.\n\n### 4. What a Human Should Check First\n\n1. **Retrieve physical samples** from Work Order 4901 produced during 06:40:32–06:40:51Z and inspect them for patches defects under standard QC procedures. This will confirm whether the camera detections represent real product defects or false positives.\n\n2. **Review Machine 1 camera and GPU logs** for 2026-07-29 06:40:32Z to identify the cause of the 1200.75 ms inference latency spike. Check for system load events, lighting adjustments, or conveyor speed changes at that timestamp.\n\n3. **Cross-reference Work Order 4901 status** with the production scheduler to confirm the order was still running at 06:40:34 UTC on 2026-07-29 and that the machine and operator assignments match camera metadata.\n\n### 5. Gaps / Missing Data\n\n- Work order timestamps in the system are dated 2026-07-07, not 2026-07-29; actual production state at burst time is unconfirmed.\n- No machine-specific downtime or maintenance records for Machine 1 in the 7-day window; unlogged stoppages or tool changes near burst time cannot be ruled out.\n- No operator logs retrieved; shift state, fatigue, or procedural adherence during the burst window are unknown.\n- No human QC defect records exist for patches on Machine 1 during this period; camera detections remain unvalidated.\n- Vision system logs (GPU load, lighting, conveyor speed) not retrieved; the inference latency anomaly cannot be fully explained without them.	2026-07-28 23:41:41-07
\.
